Here's how I'd break this project into clear phases:

Phase 1 — Core HPC problem

    Matrix multiplication / N-body simulation using MPI + OpenMP
    Image processing pipeline (parallel FFT or compression)
    Weather/climate simulation with distributed data Pick something computationally heavy so the "HPC" angle is genuine.

Phase 2 — Data pipeline on AWS S3 → Lambda (preprocess/filter) → my HPC compute → results back to S3/DynamoDB. This gives us a real data flow to demo.

Phase 3 — CI/CD + Containers Git push → Jenkins triggers → Docker image built → pushed to registry → deployed on Kubernetes. Add Ansible for provisioning the nodes. This is our DevOps showcase.

Phase 4 — Observability + Security ELK Stack for logs, Prometheus + Grafana for metrics, Wazuh for SIEM/security alerts. Hadoop security/VPN if I add Hadoop.

Phase 5 — Disaster Recovery

    Primary node failure → automatic failover to standby
    S3 snapshots for backup, DynamoDB point-in-time recovery
    Kubernetes pod rescheduling on node death
    failure+recovery demo

Suggested HPC problem statement: "Parallel climate data analysis: process satellite weather datasets stored on S3, preprocess via Lambda, run distributed simulation on Kubernetes-orchestrated MPI cluster, store results in DynamoDB, with full CI/CD, monitoring, and auto-recovery on node failure."
